<span id="Overview"></span>
== Présentation ==

Pour compiler FreeCAD avec une version spécifique d’OpenCASCADE (OCCT), assurez-vous d’abord que vous pouvez compiler FreeCAD en utilisant la version standard d’OCCT sur votre système. Ensuite, procédez comme suit :
# Récupérer le code source d’OCCT.
# Configurer OCCT.
# Compiler OCCT.
# Créer une distribution (installation) d'OCCT.
# Configurer FreeCAD.
# Compiler FreeCAD en le liant à la distribution.
# Facultatif : fusionner les commandes de compilation pour le débogage croisé FreeCAD/OCCT.

Dans la suite, je partirai du principe que vous disposez d'une installation Linux, mais la procédure est très similaire pour une installation sous Windows ou macOS.

<span id="Getting_the_OCCT_source_code"></span>
== Obtenir le code source d'OCCT ==

Dans la suite de cette page, nous partons du principe que le code source d'OCCT est stocké dans {{Incode|/home/user/occt-main}}. Dans ces conditions, exécutez la commande suivante.

{{Code|lang=bash|code=
git clone https://github.com/Open-Cascade-SAS/OCCT.git /home/user/occt-main
}}

Le code source d'OCCT se trouve dans la branche principale. À partir de là, vous pouvez facilement créer plusieurs arborescences de travail.

<span id="Configure_OCCT"></span>
== Configurer OCCT ==

Pour compiler OCCT, il est utile de définir un répertoire de compilation et un répertoire d'installation, par exemple :

{| class="wikitable"
|-
| répertoire source || {{Incode|/home/user/occt-main}}
|-
| répertoire de compilation || {{Incode|/scratch/build-occt-main}}
|-
| répertoire d'installation || {{Incode|/scratch/dist-occt-main}}
|}

Une fois que nous avons déterminé cela, nous pouvons créer un fichier {{Incode|CMakeUserPresets.json}} à la racine du répertoire source (notez que nous devons préciser explicitement le répertoire d'installation) :

{{Code|lang=json|code=
{
  "version": 3,
  "cmakeMinimumRequired": {
    "major": 3,
    "minor": 19,
    "patch": 0
  },
  "configurePresets": [
    {
      "name": "debug",
      "description": "Preset for a debug build",
      "generator": "Ninja",
      "cacheVariables": {
        "INSTALL_DIR": "/scratch/dist-occt-main",
        "CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE": "Debug",
        "CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS": "ON",
        "USE_RAPIDJSON": "ON"
      }
    }
  ]
}
}}

Maintenant que nous avons configuré OCCT, nous pouvons exécuter les commandes suivantes pour le compiler :

{{Code|lang=bash|code=
mkdir /scratch/build-occt-main
cd /scratch/build-occt-main
cmake --preset debug /home/user/occt-main
}}

<span id="Compile_OCCT"></span>
== Compiler OCCT ==

En général, je me contente de compiler avec (en supposant que je sois toujours dans le répertoire de compilation) :

{{Code|lang=bash|code=
ninja
}}

<span id="Create_a_distribution"></span>
== Créer une distribution == 

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
To create a distribution and install it in the install directory, simply execute the following command, assuming that we're still in the build directory:
</div>

{{Code|lang=bash|code=
ninja install
}}

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
We have no successfully installed OCCT in such a way that FreeCAD can link to it.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
== Configure FreeCAD ==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
It may be useful to create a worktree or a git repository that refers to the OCCT distribution that you're using, so let's assume that we have a worktree or git repository with FreeCAD's source and build directory as follows:
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
{| class="wikitable"
|-
| source directory || {{Incode|/home/user/freecad-occt-main}}
|-
| build directory || {{Incode|/scratch/build-freecad-occt-main}}
|}
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Note that we don't need an installation directory because we can directly run FreeCAD from the build directory.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Given this information, we can create a {{Incode|CMakeUserPresets.json}} that we store in FreeCAD's source code:
</div>

{{Code|lang=json|code=
{
  "version": 4,
  "configurePresets": [
    {
      "name": "occt",
      "displayName": "Debug build with custom OCCT",
      "description": "User-specific debug build with custom OCCT.",
      "inherits": [
	"common"
      ],
      "generator": "Ninja",
      "cacheVariables": {
        "CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE": "Debug",
        "FREECAD_USE_OCC_VARIANT": "Official Version",
        "CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH": "/scratch/dist-occt-main",
        "FREECAD_USE_PCL": "OFF",
        "CMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS": "ON",
        "FREECAD_QT_VERSION": "6",
        "BUILD_FEM": "OFF",
        "BUILD_OPENSCAD": "OFF",
        "BUILD_REVERSEENGINEERING": "OFF",
        "BUILD_ROBOT": "OFF",
        "BUILD_POINTS": "OFF",
        "BUILD_SMESH": "OFF",
        "BUILD_WEB": "OFF",
        "BUILD_SURFACE": "ON",
        "BUILD_INSPECTION": "OFF"
      }
    }
  ]
}
}}

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Note that we filled in the installation directory of OCCT. Some modules are turned off to diminish the compilation time as well.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Configuring FreeCAD boils down to:
</div>

{{Code|lang=bash|code=
mkdir /scratch/build-freecad-occt-main
cd /scratch/build-freecad-occt-main
cmake --preset occt /home/user/freecad-occt-main
}}

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Pay attention to the report regarding OCCT. It should have found OCCT in {{Incode|/scratch/dist-occt-main}}.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
If all of this is correct, we can compile FreeCAD.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
== Compile FreeCAD ==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Assuming we're still in the build directory, we can do:
</div>

{{Code|lang=bash|code=
ninja
}}

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
If you start FreeCAD in this directory and you check the information and libraries, FreeCAD should report the version of OCCT of your particular commit in the OCCT source.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
== (Optional) Merge the compilation commands ==
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
If you want to debug FreeCAD and OCCT at the same time, your tooling will need to be aware of the compilation commands of both FreeCAD and OCCT.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
Since you probably want to open the files from the FreeCAD source code directory {{Incode|/home/user/freecad-occt-main}}, you can create a file {{Incode|/home/user/freecad-occt-main/compile_commands.json}} that contains the compilation commands from both builds.
</div>

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
The script below can be executed as follows:
</div>

{{Code|lang=bash|code=
cd /home/user/freecad-occt-main
merge-compile-commands \
  /scratch/build-occt-main/compile_commands.json \
  /scratch/build-freecad-occt-main/compile_commands.json
}}

<div lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr">
The script {{Incode|merge-compile-commands}} should be accessible and executable:
</div>

{{Code|lang=python|code=
#!/bin/env python

import json
import os
import sys


def load(path):
    with open(path, "r") as f:
        return json.load(f)

def merge(files):
    merged = []
    for p in files:
        merged += load(p)
    return merged

if __name__ == "__main__":
    if len(sys.argv) < 3:
        print("Usage: merge-compile-commands /build1/compile_commands.json /build2/compile_commands.json")
        print("  Do this in the source tree from where you open files.")
        sys.exit(1)
    in_paths = sys.argv[1:]
    OUT_PATH = "compile_commands.json"

    if os.path.exists(OUT_PATH):
        os.remove(OUT_PATH)
    merged = merge(in_paths)
    with open(OUT_PATH, "w") as f:
        json.dump(merged, f, indent=2)
    print(f"Wrote {len(merged)} entries to {OUT_PATH}")

}}


